    <innernamespace refid="namespaceasyncdb">asyncdb</innernamespace>
    <briefdescription>
<para>Adapter between the pqxx::params produced by entity SQL builders and Drogon&apos;s incremental SQL binder. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This header declares three functions that bridge two database libraries. <ref refid="classEntity" kindref="compound">Entity</ref> code already builds its query parameters with libpqxx&apos;s pqxx::params; these functions copy those parameters into Drogon&apos;s binder so the same entity code works on both the synchronous and the asynchronous database path. One function also exposes the parameters as plain text, which is handy for tests that have no real database client.</para>
<para>WHY THIS FILE MATTERS IN THE SERVER: Whenever a controller runs SQL asynchronously it calls <ref refid="namespaceasyncdb_1abf4af65046571ee330dc2320277a57f7" kindref="member">asyncdb::execSqlCoro()</ref> from this module. That function is the only way the SQL text and its bound values reach Drogon, so this file is the seam that lets the existing entity SQL builders keep producing pqxx::params unchanged. It protects the server from SQL injection because values are bound separately from the statement text, and it preserves binary data by copying exact byte lengths. Without this adapter, every entity builder would have to be rewritten for the async path, and binary values or nulls could easily be corrupted as they cross between the two libraries.</para>
<para>CONCEPTS TO LEARN (for beginners):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Drogon DbClient and SQL binding: <ulink url="https://drogon.docsforge.com/master/database/">https://drogon.docsforge.com/master/database/</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>C++20 coroutines and co_await: <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/co_await">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/co_await</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>libpqxx parameters (pqxx::params): <ulink url="https://pqxx.org/">https://pqxx.org/</ulink></para>
